{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 54, :comments ["<b>The tender man among you and the very delicate, etc.</b> It should have said 'the tender and delicate man among you' — so why did it say 'among you' after 'tender' and 'very' after 'and delicate'? And regarding the woman it said 'and the delicate' but did not say 'very.' And further, it did not say 'the tender woman among you' but said simply 'the tender among you'; and concerning the man it said 'his eye shall be evil against his brother and against the wife of his bosom,' but concerning the woman it said 'her eye shall be evil against the husband of her bosom' and did not mention her brother. And concerning the woman it said: 'that she would eat them in lack of all things, in secret,' but concerning the man it did not mention this. And further it is difficult: what does 'his eye shall be evil' or 'her eye shall be evil' have to do with tenderness and delicacy? For is it so obvious a thing that every pampered person is grudging?" "And the explanation is: the tenderness that it mentioned is not tenderness of indulgence but tenderness of the heart, for one who is compassionate by nature has a soft heart and does not harden his heart. Therefore it says that the tender man among you — among all Israel, even toward strangers — was generous of eye, and his heart was soft to have compassion even 'among you,' among all Israel, and now his eye shall become evil even against his brother, etc. And likewise the very delicate one, who would eat nothing but delicacies — now he will eat the flesh of his sons. But the word 'among you' does not fit in this manner after 'and delicate.' And since every man by nature is not given to indulgence like a woman, therefore it was necessary to add the expression 'very' after 'and delicate,' to say that specifically the very delicate one who has gone beyond the category of other men — regarding him it is a novelty that the very delicate one should eat of the flesh of his sons, but an ordinarily delicate one — it is no novelty regarding him that he would eat of the flesh of his sons, for the man is hard by nature. But regarding the woman it is a novelty even for ordinary delicacy without 'very,' that she would eat so repulsive a thing, because the woman is in any case tender by nature with the tenderness of indulgence, as it says afterward: 'from delicacy and tenderness' — that is, tenderness of indulgence. But what is written 'the tender among you' — that is tenderness of the good eye, as stated above. And since ordinary women are grudging toward guests, and this one departed from the category of ordinary women and was compassionate — and she resembles not a woman but the way of men — therefore it did not say 'the tender woman.' And since the man has the power to give of his own to his brother but does not give, therefore it says 'his eye shall be evil against his brother'; but the woman is under others' authority and is not permitted to give her brother anything, therefore it did not say 'her eye shall be evil against her brother.' And if you say: if so, what is the meaning of what it says 'her eye shall be evil against the husband of her bosom' — how would she not give to her husband, when everything is his and how would she not give to him? — of this it says: 'that she would eat them in secret,' that she would hide the feeding from her husband; but the giving to her brother, she fears lest the matter be discovered — and perhaps her brother himself would reveal it later, for nothing remains hidden through the agency of another."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:14:23.214294534Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}